CORE
main.cpp
--cpp17 --object-bits 12
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ front-end accepted the out-of-line body: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ WRONG: must fail: FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^CONVERSION ERROR$
^found no match for symbol 'remove'$
--
N5008 [over.match.best] + [over.ics.user] + [temp.deduct] +
[class.ctor.general]: a user-defined conversion via a converting constructor is
viable based only on the source and destination types, not on the scope of the
call site.  CBMC used to reject `std::filesystem::remove(name)` (name :
std::string) with "found no match for symbol 'remove'" -> CONVERSION ERROR when
the call appeared inside an out-of-line, qualified function definition (class
member, namespace member, or static member); the identical call in a free
function at namespace scope resolved fine.  Root cause: while type-checking the
deferred out-of-line body, std::filesystem::path's constructor components were
not yet materialised, so cpp_constructor found no constructor for the
std::string->path conversion.  Fixed by resolving the constructor by the class's
own name (a constructor is named after its class) when no constructor component
is present, letting overload resolution in the class scope find it (including
constructor templates).  Reduced from src/util/tempfile.cpp
(`temporary_filet::~temporary_filet`) via cvise keeping the real headers, so it
reproduces the tempfile.cpp dog-food failure.  cleanup() is intentionally not
called (this is a front-end property); assertion.2 provides non-vacuity.
